"ERIC - EJ1469639 - A Meta-Analytic Review of the Concrete-Representational-Abstract Math Approach, Learning Disabilities Research & Practice, 2025-Feb (https://eric.ed.gov/?id=EJ1469639)\nciteturn16search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.8 years ago; Crawled: last month; A Meta-Analytic Review of the Concrete-Representational-Abstract Math Approach ... Descriptors: Meta Analysis, Mathematics Instruction, Teaching Methods, Abstract Reasoning, Problem Solving, Intervention, Instructional Effectiveness, Mathematical Concepts, Concept Formation, Effect Size, Kindergarten, Elementary Secondary Education, Databases ... Tel: 800-818-7243; Tel: 805-499-9774; Fax: 800-583-2665; e-mail: journals@sagepub.com; Web site: https://sagepub.com\n\nERIC Number: EJ1469639\n\nRecord Type: Journal\n\nPublication Date: 2025-Feb\n\nPages: 12\n\nAbstractor: As Provided\n\nISBN: N/A\n\nISSN: ISSN-0938-8982\n\nEISSN: EISSN-1540-5826\n\nAvailable Date: 0000-00-00\n\nA Meta-Analytic Review of the Concrete-Representational-Abstract Math Approach\n\nSara Ebner^{1}; Mary K. MacDonald^{1}; Paulina Grekov^{2}; Kathleen B. Aspiranti^{1}\n\nLearning Disabilities Research & Practice, v40 n1 p31-42 2025\n\nThe concrete-representational-abstract (CRA) approach is an instructional framework for teaching math wherein students move from using concrete materials to solve problems to using visual representations of the materials, and finally abstract concepts. This study provides a literature synthesis and meta-analysis of the effectiveness of the CRA approach as a math intervention. Thirty studies using single-case design methodologies were included. Descriptive analyses of the study variables are discussed. Results indicated a statistically significant overall Tau-BC effect size of 0.9965, 95% CI [0.9947, 0.9983], p < 0.0001. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA Meta-Analytic Review of the Concrete-Representational-Abstract Math Approach - Sara Ebner, Mary K. MacDonald, Paulina Grekov, Kathleen B. Aspiranti, 2025 (https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/09388982241292299)\nciteturn16search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.0 years ago; Crawled: last month; The research team considered both nonparametric and parametric effect sizes for the meta-analytic synthesis of data, specifically Tau-BC, standardized mean difference (SMD; Busk & Serlin, 1992; Gingerich, 1984), and log response ratio (LRR; Pustejovsky, 2018). ... M = male; F = female; Elem = grades K-5; Mid = Grades 6–8; High = Grades 9–12; Col = college; B = Black; H = Hispanic; W = White; CRA = concrete-representational-abstract; CRA-I = CRA-integrated; SIM = strategic instruction model; SLP = systems of least prompts; VRA = virtual-representational-abstract; VR = virtual-representational; VRA-I = VRA-integrated; VM = video modeling; R = researcher; T = teacher; DV = dependent variable; IP = independent practice; TP = timed probe; UP = untimed probe; WWC = What Works Clearinghouse Standards; M = met without reservations; MWR = met with reservations; NM = not met. ... The purpose of the current study, therefore, was to determine the effectiveness of CRA and its variations on student mathematics achievement and to investigate what role potential moderators may have on student success. ...   3. math\n  4. meta-analysis\n## Purpose of the Present Study\n\nThe purpose of the present study was to assess the effectiveness of the CRA sequence and its variations as an evidence-based mathematical intervention and to determine the role of potential moderators on student math outcomes. There were two primary research questions.\n\n1.\n\nWhat is the effectiveness of CRA and its variations on increasing student math achievement?\n\n2.\n\nWhat impact do moderators such as intervention variation, participant characteristics, and study characteristics have on the effectiveness of CRA?\n\n## Methods\n\n### Search Procedures\n\nA comprehensive search of online databases was conducted in June 2022 to identify relevant studies through the following databases: Academic Search Complete, APA PsychInfo, EBSCOhost, ERIC, Psychology and Behavioral Sciences Collection. Search keywords included “concrete representational abstract,” “concrete-representational-abstract,” “CRA,” “virtual representational abstract,” “virtual-representational-abstract,” and “VRA.” Secondary search terms “math,” “special education,” “disabilities,” and “video modeling” were entered after each key term using the Boolean operator “AND.” The search yielded a total of 1785 manuscripts; after removing duplicate titles, 1227 manuscripts remained. The titles and abstracts of these manuscripts were then reviewed, and articles that were clearly unrelated to the study were removed, leaving 73 manuscripts that underwent a full-text review.\n\nThe following criteria were used when reviewing the methods and results sections of the studies to determine final inclusion: (a) the study must have been published in English in a peer-reviewed journal; (b) the study must implement a version of the CRA/VRA intervention using an experimental design; (c) the study must provide original, quantitative data that could be extracted from the manuscript; (d) the study subjects must be students in pre-kindergarten through college in any setting; (e) the study must use a single-case experimental design. For articles that met these criteria, an ancestral search of the reference lists was conducted to locate any studies missed in the initial search. As a result, 30 articles were found to meet the inclusion criteria for data analysis (see Figure 1).\n\nImageFigure 1. PRISMA diagram of manuscript selection for Meta-analysis inclusion (Page et al., 2021).\n\n### Coding Procedures\n\nThe 30 identified articles were coded based on 49 components related to participant characteristics, methodology, intervention, dependent variables, and methodological rigor. If data were unavailable for a particular coding category, a notation of “N/A” was entered. Participant characteristics included educational setting, number of participants in the study, socioeconomic status, gender, age, race/ethnicity, and disability. Intervention characteristics included intervention name, materials used, integrated versus non-integrated approach, implementer, mnemonic used, presence of an advance/post-organizer completed during intervention, and total time spent in intervention. Amount of time was calculated based on the number of sessions and time spent in each session to determine the total minutes in intervention. Although all studies included explicit instruction techniques such as modeling and guided practice, advance or post-organizers were not used within every study. Therefore, the presence of advance/post-organizers was considered a component added to the CRA intervention instead of an imbedded component of CRA. Because this characteristic was unique to some studies it was coded as a separate component.\n\nBased on the information provided in each manuscript, dependent variable and methodology information were also coded. Using WWC (2020) standards for single-case designs, methodological rigor was assessed for all studies coded. (See the supplemental materials for the full code book.)\n\nAll studies were coded by two of the authors. Interrater reliability was calculated by dividing the number of variable agreements by the total number of variables coded: agreement across all variables coded was 95.7%. Where there were disagreements, a third author reviewed the study, and a consensus was made about the correct coding.\n\n### Data Extraction\n\nRaw data were extracted from the time-series graphs within each manuscript using WebPlotDigitizer (Rohatgi, 2015), a free web-based data extraction tool. The x-y coordinates and each data point were plotted manually for each graph, and the raw data were then exported into an Excel spreadsheet, rounded to the nearest whole number, and separated into phases that corresponded with the time-series data (i.e., baseline to intervention). The faculty mentor reviewed all extracted data with the corresponding graphs to determine any anomalies. No visual differences were found between the extracted data and graphs provided within the manuscripts.\n\n### Moderator Variables\n\nSeven moderators were selected to determine the extent to which effects of the CRA intervention varied across studies, participants, settings, and intervention variables. Moderator variables were selected on potential theoretical and logistical differences on the independent variable. Studies were coded as to whether they included students with disabilities or not. The mathematical operation variable was coded into five categories: addition, subtraction, multiplication, division, or other (e.g., fractions, Pythagorean theorem). Because several of the more recent studies incorporated virtual interventions, studies were coded if they used traditional paper-pencil and hands-on manipulatives within the intervention or whether they incorporated virtual components into the intervention. Intervention implementation was coded as whether it incorporated the concrete, representational, and abstract components within a single session (integrated) or whether concrete was taught first in separate sessions, then representational, then abstract (non-integrated). Studies were also coded whether an advance/post-organizer was included within the intervention or whether no organization tool was used. Assessment was coded as either practice problems conducted during the intervention, untimed probes prior to or after the intervention, or timed probes prior to or after the intervention.\n\n### Analysis\n\nExtracted data were recorded so that positive effect sizes represented academic improvements. Each baseline-treatment contrast was analyzed for each case within each study. A baseline-corrected Tau (Tau-BC) nonparametric effect size statistic was chosen to analyze A-B contrasts. Tau-BC measures the percentage of nonoverlapping minus overlapping data while adjusting for a linear time trend in the baseline phase (Tarlow, 2017). The research team considered both nonparametric and parametric effect sizes for the meta-analytic synthesis of data, specifically Tau-BC, standardized mean difference (SMD; Busk & Serlin, 1992; Gingerich, 1984), and log response ratio (LRR; Pustejovsky, 2018). However, due to heterogeneity in measurement procedures, particularly the presence of both count and percentage outcome types, the team determined that the log response ratio would not be appropriate, as it is only applicable for outcomes measured on a percentage or ratio scale (Pustejovsky, 2018). Upon inspecting the data, the team concluded that SMD would not be appropriate due to a lack of variability in the baseline level of participants’ data. This leads to a baseline standard deviation of zero which, by the specifications of SMD, results in the inability to estimate an effect size. Thus, the team determined that Tau-BC would be the most appropriate effect size to minimize the loss of information resulting from disregarding studies with count outcomes and participants lacking variation in the baseline phase.\n\nAll meta-analytic and meta-regression analyses were conducted in R Studio using SingleCaseES, metafor, and clubSandwich packages (Pustejovsky, 2023; Pustejovsky et al., 2023; Viechtbauer, 2010). (The R script is provided in the supplemental materials.) Tau-U statistics and the associated standard errors were first calculated for cases within each study. A multilevel, meta-analysis model with robust variance estimation that included random effects for studies and for the participants nested within studies was used (Hedges et al., 2010). Robust variance estimation techniques with small-sample corrections were used to account for statistical dependency when there were multiple outcomes per study (Hedges et al., 2010; Tipton, 2015). Robust variance estimation uses approximately inverse variance weights, and between-study variance is estimated using an assumed correlation (Hedges et al., 2010; Tipton, 2015). This model provides an overall average effect size, as well as study- and participant-level variance. The estimates of this variation provide more information regarding the extent to which effects vary within and across studies. An overall average effect size was calculated, using an intercept-only meta-regression model with robust variance estimation. This effect size represents the omnibus effect of CRA interventions. Study- and case-level effects were also calculated. Finally, moderator analyses were conducted using a mixed-effects meta-regression model with robust variance estimation.\n\n## Results\n\nA total of 30 studies met full inclusion criteria. A list and summary of studies included in the review are provided in Table 1.\n\nTable 1. Study Characteristics.\n\nStudy  | N  | M/F  | Grade  | Race  | Disability (Y/N)  | Intervention  | Integrated (Y/N)  | Implementor  | Operation  | DV  | WWC\n--- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nBouck et al. (2017)  | 3  | 2/1  | Mid  | >70% W  | Y  | VRA-I  | Y  | R  | Fractions  | IP  | MWR\nBouck et al. (2021)  | 3  | 0/3  | Elem  |    | N  | VRA with SLP  | N  | R  | Subtraction  | IP  | MWR\nBouck et al (2021b)  | 4  | 4/0  | Mid  |    | Y  | VR  | N  | R  | Multiplication Division  | IP  | MWR\nBouck et al. (2018)  | 2  | 2/0  | Mid  | >70% Multi  | Y  | VRA  | N  | R  | Addition Subtraction Multiplication Place Value  | IP  | MWR\nBouck et al (2022)  | 3  | 1/2  | Elem, Mid  |    | N  | VRA with SLP  | N  | R  | Division  | IP  | MWR\nBouck et al (2017a)  | 4  | 3/1  | Mid  |    | Y  | CRA  | N  | R  | Change Making  | IP  | M\n## Discussion\n\nCRA has been established as an evidence-based math intervention specifically when implemented with students with learning disabilities for increasing basic math achievement (Bouck et al., 2018). The current literature base lacks a cumulative review and quantitative evaluation of the effectiveness of more novel variations of CRA (e.g., CRA-I, VRA, VRA-I) and its implementation with diverse populations, settings, and target skills. The purpose of the current study, therefore, was to determine the effectiveness of CRA and its variations on student mathematics achievement and to investigate what role potential moderators may have on student success.\n\nResults indicated that, on average, CRA interventions had a large effect size (Tau-BC = 0.997) with individual-case Tau-BC values ranging from −0.82 to 1. Little variability in effects was found among the studies, and all but three showed only positive effects (Harris et al., 1995; Mancl et al., 2012; Milton et al., 2019). These data suggest that CRA is an effective intervention for increasing math skills across varying operations, types of intervention, grades, and students with and without disabilities.\n\nWhile several potential moderators were examined, only integration type was found to be significant. Non-integrated variations (i.e., CRA/VRA), where each phase is presented in separate sessions, produced greater effects than when the three phases are integrated into one session (i.e.,CRA-I/VRA-I). Integrated models initially present all phases in one session and then systematically remove the concrete and representational scaffolds as students demonstrate mastery (Flores & Hinton, 2022). This sequence has been utilized because of its potential to take less time than its non-integrated counterpart (Yakubova et al., 2016); however, CRA-I interventions were found to be less effective overall. While the difference in effect size between CRA and CRA-I was statistically significant, it is important to consider the practical significance of a difference in effect of 0.011. That is, while integrated variations may produce fewer gains, the trade-off of time versus impact may be negligible.\n\nWhen using the integrated model, several studies focused on more advanced math concepts like quadratic equations and rate of change, which could have an influence on the effectiveness of the CRA intervention. It may be more difficult to teach these advanced concepts using the CRA-I strategy than it is to teach more simple concepts such as basic addition or multiplication. Of the seven studies that used the CRA-I model, only two fully met WWC standards, one met with reservations, and four did not meet WWC standards. Therefore, the combination of an integrated approach, along with more advanced math concepts and less methodological rigor, may have contributed to the significantly lower effects of the integrated approach. Additionally, the CRA-I sequence is a newer concept and, as a result, has not yet accumulated the extensive research that the traditional CRA sequence has. As more studies implementing CRA-I are published, future research can re-evaluate its effectiveness.\n\nMost studies were conducted in Grade K-5 or Grades 6–8. This may be due to the majority (80%) of studies targeting basic math skills such as addition, subtraction, multiplication, or division, which are learned in the primary and middle grade levels. When CRA interventions were implemented with high school students, the operations targeted were typically more advanced, such as rate of change and quadratic expressions, which may be difficult to translate from an abstract to a representational or concrete level because of the inclusion of elements like quadratic coefficients, which are representational on their own. Therefore, using a manipulative like base 10 blocks or representational drawings may be time-consuming and more abstract than the CRA sequence is designed to accommodate. Additionally, with operations like rate of change or multidigit multiplication, the number of manipulatives or representative drawings required to perform the mathematical computation may be overwhelming and/or confusing to a student. However, of the studies that did report SES, most were conducted in a mid-high SES setting. Although no conclusions can be drawn from these data, the importance of identifying and implementing evidence-based interventions for specific student demographics, especially those who are affected by an educational opportunity gap, should be considered.\n\nDisability status was not a significant moderator on the effectiveness of CRA interventions. Twenty studies (67%) included participants who had a documented disability, including participants with autism, attention deficit/hyperactivity disorder, intellectual developmental disability, specific learning disability, emotional behavioral disability, and other health impairments. This suggests that CRA interventions are effective regardless of disability status and that students within special education and general education classrooms could benefit from receiving this type of intervention.\n\nAdvance organizers are designed to call attention to previous knowledge that is relevant to the content that students will learn in a lesson; that is, they relate new, unfamiliar material to something students should already know. Post-organizers follow the same principles but instead relate the new material to older/assumed knowledge after a lesson (Box & Little, 2003). The theoretical underpinnings of CRA rely on linking conceptual to procedural knowledge and, therefore, may negate the need for an advance or post-organizer. Half of all studies analyzed used some form of an advance/post-organizer, though the use of such was not found to be a significant moderator.\n\nWhile authorship was not examined as a moderator, it is important to consider. As a guideline for evidence-based practices, Horner and Kratochwill (2012) suggest interventions should be administered across at least five studies involving at least 20 participants and should be conducted by at least three independent research groups. The current analysis included 30 studies consisting of 116 participants. Of these 30 studies, 20 were produced by two research groups. Flores was an author of 12 studies, and Bouck was an author of eight studies. Additionally, both authors primarily research the non-integrated CRA sequence. This suggests that the literature on CRA interventions has undergone limited testing outside of the two primary research groups, which, therefore, might limit the generalizability of the results. While this is a concern, understanding the current results in CRA literature and their limitations is important to facilitate future research on CRA interventions.\n\n## Supplementary Material\n\nPlease find the following supplemental material available below.\n\nFor Open Access articles published under a Creative Commons License, all supplemental material carries the same license as the article it is associated with.\n\nFor non-Open Access articles published, all supplemental material carries a non-exclusive license, and permission requests for re-use of supplemental material or any part of supplemental material shall be sent directly to the copyright owner as specified in the copyright notice associated with the article.\n\n  * sj-docx-1-ldr-10.1177_09388982241292299.docx\n  * 78.63 KB\n\n#### Published In\n\nView\n\nLearning Disabilities Research & Practice\n\nVolume 40, Issue 1\n\nPages: 31 - 42\n\nArticle first published online: October 24, 2024\n\nIssue published: February 2025\n\n#### Keywords\n\n  1. concrete-representational-abstract\n  2. CRA\n  3. math\n  4. meta-analysis\n  5. single-case design\n\nSara Ebner, PhD\n\nDepartment of Educational, School, and Counseling Psychology, University of Kentucky, Lexington, KY, USA\n\nView all publications by this author\n\nMary K. MacDonald, MS\n\nDepartment of Educational, School, and Counseling Psychology, University of Kentucky, Lexington, KY, USA\n\nhttps://orcid.org/0000-0003-0202-990X\n\nView all publications by this author\n\nPaulina Grekov, MS\n\nDepartment of Education